      "content": "Temperature fluctuations can significantly affect your starter by rapidly altering the metabolic rate of both yeast and lactobacilli, leading to uneven acid production. Higher temperatures tend to favor faster yeast activity, while cooler temperatures can sometimes enhance bacterial acid production, both contributing to inconsistent pH spikes. Flour protein content primarily impacts the dough's gluten network during fermentation, providing more substrate for yeast but influencing how quickly the acids affect that structure. To stabilize your starter, try keeping a consistent temperature and ensure your flour blend is consistent for a more predictable microbial balance.",
